WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:04.580
Your business generates mountains of data,
but are you really taking advantage of the

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:07.150
insights that could reveal?

00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:13.120
You can use machine learning a branch of AI
to analyze your data and predict future outcomes

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:15.870
or identify hidden patterns.

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:21.120
Today I'll cover two approaches, namely supervised
and unsupervised machine learning.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:25.850
The big difference between the two is how
the training data is labeled.

00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:29.790
As the name suggests, supervised learning
needs guidance.

00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:32.180
We do this by using label data sets.

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:38.130
A label is simply a known value that we specify
on each row in the data set.

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:44.660
It could be something as simple as a binary
yes or no, a category or a score.

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:48.870
But what if we don't have all the information
needed to assign a helpful label?

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:52.530
Well, that's when unsupervised learning comes
into play.

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:57.379
With this approach, we use machine learning
to detect hidden patterns in data without

00:00:57.379 --> 00:00:58.379
our help.

00:00:58.379 --> 00:01:02.710
I think a few examples will help illustrate
the big differences.

00:01:02.710 --> 00:01:05.439
Let's start with supervised machine learning.

00:01:05.439 --> 00:01:10.890
Say we run multiple factories, each with robots
that need maintenance, but when exactly?

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:17.159
Well, we know from experience that the temperature
of our robots and the level of vibration affects

00:01:17.159 --> 00:01:18.579
their server schedule.

00:01:18.579 --> 00:01:21.939
So we decide to monitor those attributes.

00:01:21.939 --> 00:01:24.820
This table contains the information we've
collected.

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:31.189
The goal is to get our model to use the data
to predict the label maintenance needed, yes

00:01:31.189 --> 00:01:32.539
or no.

00:01:32.539 --> 00:01:34.860
And basically, that's supervised learning.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:40.549
We provide labeled data, and supervised learning
produces a model which can accurately predict

00:01:40.549 --> 00:01:41.639
that label.

00:01:41.639 --> 00:01:47.459
Now, when the prediction is something like
yes or no maintenance needed or not needed,

00:01:47.459 --> 00:01:50.079
we call that binary classification.

00:01:50.079 --> 00:01:52.759
Then there's multiclass classification.

00:01:52.759 --> 00:01:56.579
It's a bit of a tongue twister that indicates
any number of states.

00:01:56.579 --> 00:02:03.489
So in our robot example, a multiclass classification
might indicate if the robot needs maintenance

00:02:03.489 --> 00:02:09.539
or not, needs replacement, or just need some
rest.

00:02:09.539 --> 00:02:15.280
In other words, the multiclass approach provides
more context using our binary classification

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:16.280
model.

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:20.959
We were able to schedule maintenance on demand
by feeding real time metrics into our model

00:02:20.959 --> 00:02:25.769
and getting a simple yes or no using the multiclass
classification model.

00:02:25.769 --> 00:02:31.370
We got that and we added whether we might
need a replacement robot or could just give

00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:33.000
a robot a break.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:35.561
Another popular form of supervised machine
learning is called regression.

00:02:35.561 --> 00:02:42.060
Regression is used when you want to predict
a continuous value like temperature.

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:43.849
Back to our factory.

00:02:43.849 --> 00:02:49.129
We can train a model to predict what a robot's
temperature should be, given how hot it is

00:02:49.129 --> 00:02:53.319
in the factory and the robot's power consumption.

00:02:53.319 --> 00:02:58.920
With that model, we can compare the predicted
temperature to the robot's actual temperature.

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:03.879
Unlike our first example where we predicted
maintenance needed or not with regression,

00:03:03.879 --> 00:03:07.230
we might use a dashboard to keep an eye on
the situation.

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:09.819
Instead of getting a predictive yes or no.

00:03:09.819 --> 00:03:13.310
Okay, now we understand the basics of supervised
machine learning.

00:03:13.310 --> 00:03:15.950
Let's take a look at unsupervised machine
learning.

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:20.049
Remember, this is the approach that learns
from a data set without labels.

00:03:20.049 --> 00:03:23.069
Instead, we look for patterns in the data.

00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:26.180
There are three main types of unsupervised
learning.

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:32.349
The first is called clustering, which groups
unlabeled data based on similar characteristics.

00:03:32.349 --> 00:03:37.670
For example, an online store might use clustering
to develop customer personas.

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:40.760
That group people with similar buying patterns.

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:42.310
So how is clustering helpful?

00:03:42.310 --> 00:03:44.749
Well, take a look at this dataset.

00:03:44.749 --> 00:03:49.360
Even though this is relatively small, it's
still hard for the human eye to detect any

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:50.360
patterns.

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:55.700
Now, look at this image we produce with clustering
in this 3D diagram.

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:58.340
We've plotted out the numbers in that table.

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:04.029
Now we can easily see clusters or spot any
outliers.

00:04:04.029 --> 00:04:07.209
This has major implications for online retailers.

00:04:07.209 --> 00:04:11.560
Using this model you can tailor your user
experience for each group.

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:18.389
You can also flag customer behavior that doesn't
fit any persona and could be identity fraud.

00:04:18.389 --> 00:04:23.250
The next type of unsupervised machine learning
is called association.

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:27.660
Association is used to identify relationships
in the data.

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:32.789
Picture your music streaming playlist when
your platform pops up a message.

00:04:32.789 --> 00:04:38.000
Listeners who liked X also liked Y that's
association.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:41.530
Finally, there's dimensionality reduction.

00:04:41.530 --> 00:04:46.890
It helps eliminate noisy, redundant data from
unmanageable datasets.

00:04:46.890 --> 00:04:51.240
This reduction simplifies the input data before
training a model.

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:56.530
It's sort of a smart, tactical way to trim
fat and actually get more accurate results

00:04:56.530 --> 00:04:58.240
with less data.

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:04.301
Okay, so that's my brief overview of supervised
versus unsupervised machine learning, but

00:05:04.301 --> 00:05:08.000
the question remains, which approach is right
for you?

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:12.960
If you want to predict outcomes and you're
willing to train your model by manually labeling

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:16.680
your data, supervised learning is your best
bet.

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:21.889
In our factory example, knowing what robots
needed servicing reduced downtime with proactive

00:05:21.889 --> 00:05:27.199
and cost effective maintenance, but if you
have lots of data and are struggling to identify

00:05:27.199 --> 00:05:30.419
patterns, go with unsupervised learning.

00:05:30.419 --> 00:05:34.919
It can help clear the fog that's preventing
you from seeing what the data is telling you.

00:05:34.919 --> 00:05:39.940
In our simple music shopping example, grouping
shared interests helped improve the customer

00:05:39.940 --> 00:05:43.310
experience and boosted sales.

00:05:43.310 --> 00:05:46.030
When you're ready to start, think of as an
experiment.

00:05:46.030 --> 00:05:48.449
There are many different tools you can try.

